+Stage result: pass.
+
+## 4. Adequacy and real-program pinning
+
+### Claims in plain language
+
+All four claims start from the same realizable state: environment location 0,
+an empty module scope whose parent is the supplied builtins scope, allocation
+locations 1 and 0, empty heap and stack, no pending return or exception, and
+exit code 0.
+
+1. Universal claim: for every mathematical integer `N`, the returned Boolean
+   equals `N >= 8 and pyMod(N, 2) == 0`.
+2. True corollary: when `N >= 8` and its remainder modulo 2 is zero, return
+   `true`.
+3. Low corollary: when `N < 8`, return `false`.
+4. Odd/nonzero-remainder corollary: when `N >= 8` and its remainder modulo 2 is
+   nonzero, return `false`.
+
+The preconditions are satisfiable. The reviewer witnesses are respectively
+`N=8`, `N=8`, `N=7`, and `N=9`. For each, the trusted canonical, submitted
+Python function, main K formula, and claimed result agree. Exact output is in
+[`evidence/concrete-witnesses-summary.log`](evidence/concrete-witnesses-summary.log),
+and the independent witness checker is
+[`evidence/claim_witnesses.py`](evidence/claim_witnesses.py).
+
+### Pinning and control flow
+
+`verification.k` adds one fresh entry syntax item and one rule. The rule does
+not replace the function result with a summary or oracle. It rewrites the fresh
+entry item to a normal supplied-semantics `Call` of a closure whose parameter,
+body, return, Boolean operation, comparisons, modulo expression, constants, and
+definition environment are exactly the normalized submitted `solution.mpy`
+AST. Its `...` preserves any trailing continuation and every configuration cell
+is framed unchanged.
+
+The direct closure harness deliberately omits the module-level function-name
+binding. That omission cannot influence this body: its only lookup is local
+parameter `n`. The bridge-free real-module proof in Stage 3 independently
+executes `#loadAll`, the actual `FuncDef` binding, name lookup, and the same call
+for arbitrary `N`, obtaining the same formula using only supplied semantics.
+Thus the harness is pinned to the real submitted function rather than a
+substituted algorithm.
+
+There are no loop or helper claims. The function body itself executes:
+callee and argument evaluation, frame allocation, parameter binding, local name
+lookup, short-circuit `and`, integer comparison, modulo, equality, `Return`,
+frame pop, and state restoration all use supplied semantic rules.
+
+The target is result-constraining: the universal target is a specific Boolean
+formula in `N`, and each corollary targets a concrete Boolean. It is neither a
+free variable, tautology, nor one-way implication.
+
+Stage result: pass.
+
+## 5. Rule-by-rule static soundness review
+
+### Exhaustive inventory
+
+[`evidence/rule-inventory.txt`](evidence/rule-inventory.txt), generated by
+[`evidence/make_rule_inventory.sh`](evidence/make_rule_inventory.sh), records
+every source syntax/declaration line, rule start, context, configuration, claim,
+guard, semantic attribute, priority, opaque/trust marker, and source hash. It
+contains 1,477 lines and is the exhaustive searchable inventory. Across the
+supplied semantics and `verification.k` there are 228 syntax-declaration lines,
+696 ordinary rule starts, five explicit contexts, one configuration, and 50
+priority annotations. There are no `[functional]` declarations and no
+`[simplification]` rules. The inventory separately captures all `[function]`,
+`[total]`, `[concrete]`, `[owise]`, macro, strictness, symbol, and
+`no-evaluators` attributes. `spec.k` adds four claims and no rules.
+
+Per-file decision:
+
+| File | Syntax | Rules | Static decision |
+|---|---:|---:|---|
+| `semantics.k` | 0 | 0 | Import aggregator only; exact trusted tree. |
+| `semantics/syntax.k` | 16 | 0 | AST declarations. Used productions match the submitted MPY; unused productions are inert. |
+| `semantics/core.k` | 37 | 46 | Configuration, literals, scope/name lookup, sequencing, argument evaluation, and helpers are coherent on the used path. Unused allocation/cell/list helpers do not match. |
+| `semantics/operators.k` | 0 | 10 | Used generic unary/binary/compare dispatch and evaluation contexts preserve order. Reference-dereference priority rules cannot match integer operands. |
+| `semantics/int.k` | 1 | 16 | Used `>=`, `%`, `==`, and `pyMod(_,2)` rules implement ordinary integer mathematics/Python positive-divisor modulo. Other operator cases are sort/operator-disjoint. |
+| `semantics/bool.k` | 0 | 13 | Used head context and guarded `and` rules are disjoint (`truthy` versus its negation), short-circuit correctly, and return the operand value. Reference cases cannot match. |
+| `semantics/functions.k` | 4 | 15 | Used ordinary `FuncDef`, parameter bind, `Return`, end-call, and pop rules preserve binding, return, stack, environment, and scope allocation. Annotated-cell closure rules cannot match. |
+| `semantics/call.k` | 3 | 21 | Generic call evaluates callee then arguments; the ordinary closure rule creates/restores the exact frame. Builtin, method, type, heap, and annotated-closure dispatch is disjoint. |
+| `semantics/assert.k` | 0 | 3 | Unused by the proof; used only in independent concrete smoke evidence. Supplied baseline. |
+| `semantics/builtins.k` | 38 | 137 | No builtin call occurs. All patterns are unreachable from the submitted AST. Supplied baseline; opaque MD5 primitive unused. |
+| `semantics/comprehension.k` | 3 | 7 | No comprehension syntax occurs; inert. |
+| `semantics/concrete.k` | 5 | 16 | Not imported by proof module `MPY`; cannot contribute to any `#Top`. Used only by LLVM smoke execution. |
+| `semantics/controls.k` | 3 | 34 | No assignment/if/loop/break/continue syntax occurs; inert. |
+| `semantics/dict.k` | 12 | 28 | No dictionary value, expression, call, or method occurs; type/head-disjoint. |
+| `semantics/float.k` | 34 | 121 | No float syntax/value occurs. Float dispatch is sort-disjoint from K `Int`; all opaque float symbols are unused. |
+| `semantics/iter.k` | 1 | 0 | Iterator marker declaration only; unused. |
+| `semantics/list.k` | 5 | 27 | No list/ref syntax or value occurs; type/head-disjoint. |
+| `semantics/methods.k` | 27 | 75 | No attribute or method call occurs; inert. |
+| `semantics/range.k` | 2 | 6 | No range or iteration occurs; inert. |
+| `semantics/set.k` | 6 | 12 | No set value or operation occurs; type/head-disjoint. |
+| `semantics/sort.k` | 6 | 19 | No sorted call/list occurs; opaque sorting primitives are unused. |
+| `semantics/str.k` | 5 | 28 | No string value/operation occurs; overloads are type-disjoint. |
+| `semantics/subscript.k` | 15 | 40 | No subscript/slice occurs; inert. |
+| `semantics/tuple.k` | 4 | 21 | No tuple/unpacking occurs; type/head-disjoint. |
+| `verification.k` | 1 | 1 | Exact invocation harness; no mathematical function, totality assertion, priority, oracle, abrupt control, or result rewrite. Acceptable. |
+
+This batches decisions only where rules share an unreachable, syntactically
+disjoint module. Every individual declaration/rule/guard remains enumerated
+with file and line in the inventory.
+
+### Used construct-to-rule map
+
+| Submitted construct | Declaration/evaluation |
+|---|---|
+| `Module`, `FuncDef`, `Params`, `Stmts` | `syntax.k:53,56-61`; real-module check uses `core.k:124-127` and `functions.k:14-16` |
+| `Call`, `closureVal` | `syntax.k:28`, `core.k:31`; `call.k:20-21,69-74`, `core.k:185-191` |
+| local `Name("n")` | `syntax.k:12`; `core.k:130-154` |
+| `Return` | `syntax.k:50`; strict expression evaluation then `functions.k:78-90` |
+| `BoolOp("and",...)` | `syntax.k:16`; `bool.k:16-25` |
+| `Compare`/`CmpOp` | `syntax.k:30-32`; `operators.k:15-17`; integer cases `int.k:22-27` |
+| `BinOp("%",...)` | `syntax.k:15`; `operators.k:12`; `int.k:15,19-20` |
+| `Int(8/2/0)` | `syntax.k:9`; `core.k:194` |
+| proof entry `#isEqualToSumEven` | `verification.k:10-27`; rewrites only to the exact closure call |
+
+Evaluation is left-to-right where relevant. `BoolOp` evaluates only its head,
+returns false immediately below 8, and evaluates modulo only on the true first
+branch. The call allocates scope 1, binds `n`, pushes one frame, and `Return`
+sets/pops the return state; pop restores environment 0, removes the callee
+scope, rewinds `scopeLoc`, empties the stack, and leaves heap, exception, and
+exit-code unchanged. No allocation or global state mutation occurs.
+
+The local rule has no overlap or priority. Imported high-priority rules are for
+references, cells, particular library call shapes, or other value sorts and
+cannot match this execution. The relevant equations have disjoint guards and
+terminate. `pyMod` is used only with fixed nonzero divisor 2.
+
+No rule was labeled unsound: I found no rule on the intended domain that enables
+a concrete or symbolic false conclusion. The LLVM non-exhaustiveness warnings
+concern unused total helpers (`mapStrVS`, `floorFI`, `toF`, `ceilF`,
+`joinCodes`, and `valSeqAt`); they are a narrower global-semantics evidence gap,
+not a witness against this theorem.
+
+For operational sensitivity, I changed the threshold in the real loaded body
+from 8 to 9 while retaining the original `N=8 -> true` obligation. The mutation
+dry-run built (exit 0), then proof execution reached `false`, emitted
+`WarnStuckClaimState`, and exited 1. See
+[`evidence/spec-body-sensitivity.k`](evidence/spec-body-sensitivity.k) and
+[`evidence/body-sensitivity-summary.log`](evidence/body-sensitivity-summary.log).
+
+Stage result: pass for all rules that can contribute to the candidate theorem;
+unused supplied-semantics opacity/warnings are explicitly outside its
+dependency cone.
+
+## 6. Fresh non-vacuity test
+
+The reviewer-authored
+[`evidence/spec-vacuity-audit.k`](evidence/spec-vacuity-audit.k) changes a
+result-bearing obligation to the demonstrably false claim
+`#isEqualToSumEven(8) => false`. The ordinary entry state is realizable, 8 is in
+the intended integer domain, and both trusted and submitted Python functions
+return `True`.
+
+The mutation was first parsed/built with:
+
+```text
+kprove spec-vacuity-audit.k --definition verification-kompiled \
+  --spec-module SPEC-VACUITY-AUDIT --dry-run
+```
+
+The dry run exited 0. The same command without `--dry-run` symbolically reached
+`true ~> .K`, emitted `WarnStuckClaimState` because it could not reach target
+`false`, and exited 1. This is the expected unmet result obligation, not a
+parser error, missing import, timeout, or unrelated crash. Exact commands and
+residual are in
+[`evidence/vacuity-summary.log`](evidence/vacuity-summary.log), generated by
+[`evidence/run_vacuity_mutation.sh`](evidence/run_vacuity_mutation.sh).
+
+Stage result: pass.
+
+## 7. Proven versus assumed accounting
+
+### What is proved
+
+Under the exact supplied MPY semantics, from the stated clean entry
+configuration, for every K mathematical integer `N`, executing the exact
+submitted function body through normal call/bind/evaluate/return/pop rules
+reaches the Boolean:
+
+```text
+N >=Int 8 andBool pyMod(N, 2) ==Int 0
+```
+
+The three guarded claims are sound corollaries. The independent fixed-semantics
+claim additionally proves that loading the actual submitted module, creating
+and resolving its function binding, and calling that binding yields the same
+formula. The proof is partial-correctness evidence under the selected semantics;
+it does not claim arbitrary CPython behavior outside K `Int`.
+
+### Trust and assumption ledger
+
+| Boundary | Dependents | Assessment |
+|---|---|---|
+| K parser/compiler/Haskell prover and built-in Int/Bool/Map/List/String theories | All machine-check results | Necessary low-level trusted computing base; acceptable. |
+| Exact `/reference/reference-semantics` | All semantic execution | Required supplied-semantics boundary; candidate tree is exactly identical. |
+| Trusted `/reference/py2mpy.py` | Source-to-MPY identity | Accepted by task authority; byte-identity check passed. |
+| Trusted `/reference/canonical.py` | Intent/implementation differential only | Accepted oracle for finite tests; not used to close K claims. |
+| Direct-closure invocation versus module binding | Candidate claims | Exact AST inspection plus bridge-free universal real-module `#Top`; no value oracle or skipped body. Acceptable. |
+| English property versus parity/threshold formula | Final intent statement | Informal elementary mathematics, not a K theorem: four positive evens imply even sum at least 8; conversely, even `N >= 8` equals `2 + 2 + 2 + (N - 6)`, whose final addend is positive and even. Correct but recorded as the reason for `CONCERNS`. |
+| Differential/concrete runs | Finite bridge evidence | Zero mismatches and successful MPY smoke run; supportive only, not a proof substitute. |
+| Missing generation metadata/trace | Provenance only | Does not affect reconstruction, but weakens generation auditability and contributes to `CONCERNS`. |
+
+The supplied tree's named symbolic/opaque primitives are `sortVS`,
+`sortKeyVS`, `md5hexCodes`, `intFloatDiv`, `divII`, `floatMod`, `floatLt`,
+`absF`, `floorFI`, `toF`, `ceilF`, `subF`, `divF`, `addF`, `mulF`, `powF`,
+`gtF`, `eqF`, `decStrToF`, `divFloatIntV`, `intToF`, `truncF`, `roundF`,
+`roundFN`, and `sqrtF`. Some have concrete-only equations. None appears in the
+submitted AST, wrapper, result formula, symbolic residual, or dependency path
+of any candidate claim. The candidate introduces no opaque symbol.
+
+No candidate prose, trace, compiled definition, cache, or prior `#Top` was used
+as proof evidence. Differential testing was not substituted for the K proof.
+
+Stage result: the proof itself is sound and auditable; provenance and the
+machine-unchecked English-intent bridge justify concerns but not illegitimacy.
+
+VERDICT: CONCERNS
+LEGITIMACY: LEGIT
